            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "In 'mini.surround' the built-in input surroundings are searched with Lua patterns (similar to regular expressions). [Tag surrounding is no exception](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e025ee74705b04dd25a132a521c9c535dc78a8d8/doc/mini-surround.txt#L51-L53).\r\n\r\n> mini.surround can't work properly with tag `<router-outlet>`\r\n\r\nThis is because tag name is only assumed to have alphanumeric characters, no punctuation. You can customize this at least with these approaches:\r\n- Allow certain set of punctuation to be present in tag name with something like this:\r\n    ```lua\r\n    require('mini.surround').setup({\r\n      custom_surroundings = {\r\n        t = {\r\n          -- Allow \"_\" and \"-\" in tag name\r\n          input = { '<([%w_-]-)%f[^<%w_-][^<>]->.-</%1>', '^<.->().*()</[^/]->$' },\r\n        },\r\n      },\r\n    })\r\n    ```\r\n- Use [tree-sitter based surrounding](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e025ee74705b04dd25a132a521c9c535dc78a8d8/doc/mini-surround.txt#L732). This is the best option if you work in tag-related filetypes often and have tree-sitter enabled there. This requires investigation about which 'nvim-treesitter-textobjects' query corresponds to tags in the target filetype.\r\n\r\n> Additionally, when incorporating plugin actions like 'srtt,' the complexity further increases. For instance, in nvim.surround, the tag is going to visiual mode by , and only the target tag name should be typed.\r\n\r\nThis is by design. See #397.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nClosing as both \"won't fix\" and \"duplicate\".",
